  ⚠ Remaining incompatibilities noted by connector LLM:
    - minimalmodbus's Instrument is fundamentally serial-port oriented; using it for Modbus TCP relies on an undocumented/legacy MODE_TCP path that is not a 
supported, robust TCP client and effectively requires third-party extensions or workarounds outside the core library's intended use — this is a structural fit 
problem, not something glue code can truly resolve, only paper over.
    - The read/write and connection lifecycle model differs enough (lazy-opening Instrument vs explicit connect(), exception-raising vs isError() response 
objects, address bound to Instrument vs passed per-call) that while a wrapper can approximate the old API surface, it cannot guarantee equivalent TCP 
reliability/timeout/reconnect behavior that pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient provided natively.
    - Because minimalmodbus was designed for serial/RS-485 use, its TCP support is not an officially maintained, first-class feature — any TCP behavior 
(multi-slave addressing, socket reconnection, timeouts) achieved via wrapper is best-effort emulation, not a real migration of the ModbusIO's TCP client 
contract.

